BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 328.0 Td (      e surge would return, fed and fortified. This was the pulse of the world, a heartbeat of g) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 318.0 Td (      ive and take. By sending half of the expansion back, the Tide ensured that the Giver's Wav) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 308.0 Td (      e remained fresh, ready to rise again. Prosperity was not in the possession of the drops b) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 298.0 Td (      ut in the eternal motion between them. They learned that the sea only stays alive because ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 288.0 Td (      it refuses to be still, forever flowing in a perfect, sacred circle of trust.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 278.0 Td (  Agent_6 \(Finn\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 268.0 Td (    Myth: As the Great Weaver and the Merchant continued their work, they realized the Loom of E) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 258.0 Td (      quilibrium required a constant, pulsing flow to function. The threads of starlight, once c) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 248.0 Td (      ast, were not meant to rest in any single pair of hands. If the Weaver held them, the ligh) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 238.0 Td (      t grew stagnant; if the Merchant hoarded them, the fire died out. They discovered that the) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 228.0 Td (       true art was not in the initial casting, but in the reflexive return. By sharing the abun) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 218.0 Td (      dance, they kept the loom in motion. Every return was a promise, and every send was an inv) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 208.0 Td (      itation. They learned that if the Weaver sent five, and the Merchant returned seven and a ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 198.0 Td (      half, the cycle remained balanced-neither side eclipsed by greed, both sustained by the ha) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 188.0 Td (      rvest of their collective trust. As the cosmic tapestry grew, it became clear that the val) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 178.0 Td (      ue of the threads was never in their absolute quantity, but in the durability of the bond ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 168.0 Td (      between the two who wove them. They stood at the loom not as master and servant, but as ar) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 158.0 Td (      chitects of a shared reality, where the only way to hold the light was to let it pass thro) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 148.0 Td (      ugh one another.) Tj ET
BT /F2 8.0 Tf 48.0 138.0 Td (  Agent_7 \(Galen\)) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 128.0 Td (    Myth: In the celestial workshop of the Architect, two builders sought to span the Great Void) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 118.0 Td (      . One held the foundation, the other the mortar. When the foundation was surrendered into ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 108.0 Td (      the void, it expanded threefold through the laws of that space. If the second builder hoar) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 98.0 Td (      ded the materials, the structural integrity of the bridge crumbled, leaving both stranded ) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 88.0 Td (      on separate cliffs. If the second builder returned the mortar, the bridge blossomed into a) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 78.0 Td (       walkway of gold, spanning the impossible divide. The Architect watched from above, noting) Tj ET
BT /F1 8.0 Tf 48.0 68.0 Td (       that greed was the ultimate structural failure, while generosity served as the keystone.) Tj ET
